---
const neurons = Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => ({
  x: 164 + (i % 4) * 49,
  y: 146 + Math.floor(i / 4) * 46,
}));
---

<div class="talos-architecture" data-talos-story>
  <figure class="talos-architecture-diagram">
    <svg
      class="architecture-desktop"
      viewBox="0 0 480 450"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="architecture-title architecture-desc"
    >
      <title id="architecture-title">Talos 1: input, sixteen stored states, one shared update unit, and host readout</title>
      <desc id="architecture-desc">
        Eight input bits arrive over SPI. Sixteen neurons store signed eight-bit
        membrane values. A single arithmetic unit updates them in sequence using
        a frozen snapshot of previous spikes. The host reads the completed state
        and applies a separate learned readout.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <marker
          id="talos-arrow"
          markerWidth="7"
          markerHeight="7"
          refX="6"
          refY="3.5"
          orient="auto"
        >
          <path
            d="M0 0L7 3.5L0 7"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.4"
          />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="0">
        <rect x="18" y="21" width="116" height="61" rx="12" />
        <text x="76" y="47">
          HOST / SPI
        </text>
        <text class="diagram-small" x="76" y="66">
          8 input bits
        </text>
      </g>
      <path class="architecture-wire" d="M134 52H238V110" />
      <path
        class="architecture-signal"
        data-input-signal
        d="M134 52H238V110"
        pathLength="100"
      />
      <rect
        class="architecture-chip"
        x="119"
        y="105"
        width="245"
        height="251"
        rx="20"
      />
      <text class="diagram-small" x="242" y="126">
        TALOS 1 · 16 STORED STATES
      </text>
      <g class="architecture-recurrence" data-recurrence>
        <path d="M164 146L213 192L262 238L311 284L164 284L213 238L262 192L311 146" />
        <path d="M376 281Q418 190 376 148" marker-end="url(#talos-arrow)" />
        <text
          class="diagram-small"
          x="408"
          y="220"
          transform="rotate(-90 408 220)"
        >
          previous spikes
        </text>
      </g>
      {neurons.map((n, i) => (
        <g class="architecture-neuron" data-neuron={i}>
          <circle cx={n.x} cy={n.y} r="16" />
          <text x={n.x} y={n.y + 4}>
            {i}
          </text>
        </g>
      ))}
      <g class="architecture-cursor" data-scan-cursor style="display: none">
        <rect x="143" y="125" width="42" height="42" rx="10" />
      </g>
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="3">
        <rect x="144" y="312" width="196" height="31" rx="8" />
        <text x="242" y="332">
          ONE SHARED LIF UNIT
        </text>
      </g>
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="2">
        <rect x="18" y="164" width="83" height="99" rx="10" />
        <text x="59" y="191">
          FROZEN
        </text>
        <text x="59" y="209">
          SPIKES
        </text>
        <text class="diagram-small" x="59" y="235">
          last step
        </text>
      </g>
      <path class="architecture-wire" d="M101 215H119M242 356V382" />
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="4">
        <rect x="144" y="385" width="196" height="53" rx="12" />
        <text x="242" y="406">
          HOST READOUT
        </text>
        <text class="diagram-small" x="242" y="424">
          state → prediction
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <svg
      class="architecture-mobile"
      viewBox="0 0 360 185"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Talos architecture: SPI input, sixteen neuron states, frozen previous spikes, one shared LIF unit, and host readout"
    >
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="0">
        <rect x="5" y="10" width="86" height="46" rx="8" />
        <text x="48" y="30">
          HOST / SPI
        </text>
        <text class="diagram-small" x="48" y="46">
          8 input bits
        </text>
      </g>
      <path class="architecture-wire" d="M91 33H111" />
      <rect
        class="architecture-chip"
        x="112"
        y="5"
        width="241"
        height="123"
        rx="12"
      />
      <text x="232" y="23">
        16 NEURON STATES
      </text>
      {neurons.map((_, i) => (
        <g class="architecture-neuron" data-neuron={i}>
          <circle
            cx={133 + (i % 8) * 28}
            cy={43 + Math.floor(i / 8) * 26}
            r="10"
          />
          <text
            class="diagram-small"
            x={133 + (i % 8) * 28}
            y={46 + Math.floor(i / 8) * 26}
          >
            {i}
          </text>
        </g>
      ))}
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="3">
        <rect x="125" y="91" width="215" height="26" rx="6" />
        <text x="232" y="108">
          ONE SHARED LIF UNIT
        </text>
      </g>
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="2">
        <rect x="5" y="85" width="86" height="55" rx="8" />
        <text x="48" y="105">
          FROZEN
        </text>
        <text x="48" y="121">
          SPIKES
        </text>
        <text class="diagram-small" x="48" y="134">
          last step
        </text>
      </g>
      <path class="architecture-wire" d="M91 111H112M232 128V148" />
      <g class="architecture-block" data-block="4">
        <rect x="112" y="150" width="241" height="29" rx="8" />
        <text x="232" y="169">
          HOST READOUT → PREDICTION
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      <span data-diagram-caption>
        Input bits travel from the host to Talos over SPI.
      </span>
      <span class="diagram-note">
        Architecture schematic · scroll follows the explanation
      </span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="talos-architecture-text">
    <section data-architecture-step="0">
      <h3>1. Send an event</h3>
      <p>
        A host computer or microcontroller sends{' '}
        <strong>eight input bits over SPI</strong>, a simple serial connection.
        Each bit can directly nudge one of neurons 0–7. It is a tiny doorbell
        panel: the bits say which bells rang, without saying what the pattern
        means.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section data-architecture-step="1">
      <h3>2. Keep sixteen little memories</h3>
      <p>
        Each of the <strong>sixteen neurons</strong> stores an 8-bit signed
        number, from −128 to +127. Incoming events add to it; a leak subtracts a
        little. If the result reaches a threshold, the neuron emits a one-bit{' '}
        <strong>spike</strong> and resets to zero. That is “leaky
        integrate-and-fire,” or LIF. A number bucket with a leak and an overflow
        bell.
      </p>
      <p>
        The stored number is the <strong>membrane value</strong>. The spike is a
        separate yes-or-no event. Talos exposes both: a bucket can contain
        useful information even when its bell has stayed quiet.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section data-architecture-step="2">
      <h3>3. Let yesterday nudge today</h3>
      <p>
        The network is <strong>recurrent</strong>: spikes from the previous step
        feed back into the next update. Its fixed, ring-like wiring includes
        excitatory and inhibitory connections and a connection five positions
        away. Connection signs are −1, 0, or +1, scaled by a shared setting. Add
        or subtract; no general-purpose multiplier required.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the start of each step, the circuit{' '}
        <strong>freezes the previous spike vector</strong>. Every neuron reads
        that same snapshot. Otherwise a neuron updated later could peek at this
        step’s new spikes. That would make the answer depend on who got their
        turn first.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can adjust the common weight, threshold, leak, and input bits. The
        wiring itself is fixed in the Verilog; SPI cannot redraw it.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section data-architecture-step="3">
      <h3>4. Pass the calculator</h3>
      <p>
        Sixteen stored values; <strong>one shared LIF arithmetic unit</strong>.
        A sequencer visits neurons 0, 1, 2 … 15. One time-step takes sixteen
        update cycles after it starts. Think of a calculator passed down a row:
        less hardware, more waiting. The orange outline follows those turns as
        you scroll.
      </p>
      <p>
        During the update, <code>busy</code> is high and another step trigger is
        ignored. The host waits until it clears before reading a completed
        result. The spike bits change during the scan, so reading halfway
        through would mix unfinished work with finished work.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section data-architecture-step="4">
      <h3>5. Read the pattern outside the chip</h3>
      <p>
        The host reads the final spikes and membrane values. A separate software{' '}
        <strong>readout</strong> can learn to turn that changing state into a
        prediction. The chip makes the pattern; the host interprets it. Drummer,
        then song recognizer.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why the circuit is called a <strong>reservoir</strong>. It
        supplies a changing internal state rather than a complete trained
        classifier. It does not train itself or know what a gesture means. The
        benchmark results later in this post evaluate simulations and host-side
        readouts.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
<script>
  import { startTalosStory } from '../scene/talos-story';
  startTalosStory();
</script>
